*.lock
run/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.bak
# Lane-created pre-edit backups (e.g. tools/oc-x.pre-20260925-115731). Generated
# residue: they accumulate and nothing reaps them.
#
# MEASURED 2026-11-19 (c29 lens D) — the die-7 claim this comment used to carry was
# FALSE, and it propagated into a work order before anyone checked it. `oc-ledger:1540`
# builds its stray set from plain `git status --porcelain`, which OMITS ignored paths,
# so `*.bak` and `*.pre-*` are invisible to it: clutter, never blockers. The one sidecar
# class that CAN block a version bump is `*.bak-*`, because the `*.bak` line above does
# not match it and it is therefore not ignored (0 live 2026-11-19, which is why no bump
# has tripped). A reaper that sweeps `*.bak` alone misses the only class that matters.
*.pre-*

# The battery red-receipt transcript (#517/#614). Since #614 the receipt embeds
# its own evidence (fail_rows), so this file is a transient human-readable
# transcript, not a durable artifact -- and being untracked it tripped the
# sync stray-guard (die 7) while a red was under investigation.
tools/tests/battery-last-fail.log

# The battery's reap-probe sidecar (`tools/tests/.reap.<pid>`, run.sh:85-87).
# The NORMAL path deletes it on the very next line; a KILLED run skips that, so
# every killed battery leaves a zero-byte untracked file behind -- and untracked
# paths are exactly what the sync stray-guard (die 7) refuses. MEASURED
# 2026-11-20: two zero-byte sidecars from the 11:32 and 12:06 runs (both killed)
# sat in the tree and would have blocked the 0.4.324 bump; the `rm -f` at
# run.sh:87 never ran for either. Ignoring the class is non-destructive -- the
# residue stays on disk, it just stops reading as another actor's dirty work.
tools/tests/.reap.*

# Runtime review artefacts (Duty-4 proposals, Duty-6 reports, evidence).
# These belong to the STATE repo (opencrabs-dev/reviews/), which is where the
# intake lane reads and where oc-ledger's TRACKED sweep covers them. Lanes that
# write them into the SKILL repo instead create an untracked path -- and because
# the sync stray-guard reads a full `git status --porcelain`, ONE lane's misplaced
# proposal directory blocks every version bump with die 7. Live instance
# 2026-09-27: reviews/20260927-c25/ held 7 files, 6 of them duplicates of state-repo
# copies and ONE (telegram-95bec69b-1.md) with no counterpart anywhere -- so both
# "delete" and "rehome" would have destroyed a real submission. Ignoring the class
# is non-destructive: the stray file stays on disk and on someone's record.
reviews/

# pytest's own bookkeeping. The RECEIPT is deliberately NOT here: pytest writes
# the tracked `tools/tests/battery-last.json`, because that exact path is the
# fleet's version-bump gate (`oc-ledger sync` reads it, dies rc 5 on a missing,
# stale or RED verdict, and names it in its message). An ignored receipt would
# leave the gate reading a verdict nothing refreshed — green while the suite is
# red. A partial run clobbering it is a known hazard: factory #214.
tools/tests/pytest/.pytest_cache/
